    "questionText": "(I) Occasionally littered with calcified corpses, the bright red water of Lake Natron in Tanzania gives a very clear warning to any life form that approaches its shores. (II) As its source, the volcano Mount Ol Doinyo Lengai releases run-off water from its slopes, mixing it with volcanic material. (III) As a result, the lake's water becomes highly salty and alkaline, which severely limits the number of animal and plant species that can survive in the waters. (IV) If an animal dies in these waters, there is a high chance that it will become calcified as the salty lake hardens its body. (V) However, one organism that thrives in its salty waters is the extremophile cyanobacteria, which is the red algae that colours the lake and makes it toxic to other life forms.",
