    "passage": "The power of scent to unlock memories and ease distress in dementia patients is a growing focus of clinical neuroscience. Exposure to a personally meaningful fragrance, like a perfume someone wore for decades, has been shown to reduce some of the symptoms of dementia, including agitation and restlessness. Ellen Cole and her colleagues at the University of Oregon Health Centre suggest that scents dementia patients find personally significant may calm their distress by activating the olfactory-limbic pathway — the neural route that connects the sense of smell directly to the brain's emotional centre and is one of the few pathways relatively spared by dementia. Researchers have shown that stimulating this pathway with a self-selected fragrance seems to reactivate neighbouring regions of the brain that have been damaged by the disease. With the help of brain scans, the researchers found that patients exposed to their personal scent exhibited significantly higher functional connectivity in the limbic, olfactory, and memory-related networks compared to patients exposed to a neutral, unfamiliar scent. Yet, the researchers stress that the small sample size of 21 patients and the narrow scope of the study mean that the results are not conclusive. The study only included a single scanning session for each patient, so it remains unclear whether the effects identified in the study persist beyond a brief period. “No one is claiming that scent therapy will cure dementia, but it might make the symptoms more manageable,” says Cole.",
    "questionText": "What is the primary purpose of the author?",
