    "passage": "Hundreds of parchment rolls from a monastery library, charred by a fire in 1731, constitute one of the few surviving collections of early modern administrative records in the region. Researchers are using advanced imaging technologies and machine learning to reveal writings that have remained unreadable for nearly three centuries. Previous attempts to unroll the parchments were destructive. Today, non-invasive methods that involve high-resolution X-ray micro-computed tomography (micro-CT) can produce detailed, digital images of the internal structures of the rolls without further damaging them. Identifying the location of ink on the parchment, which would reveal the written text, is challenging because both the parchment and the iron-gall ink have similar density: there is little visible contrast between the two, making the text seem invisible. However, machine learning may be able to discern what is difficult for the human eye. Researchers point the artificial intelligence (AI) to areas of known ink coverage to study those areas. Then, AI applies what it learns to new areas where no contrast can be detected by the human eye to reveal writing not seen for centuries. The project resulted in 1,500 words of previously unread text — a record probably kept by a local notary. What is most striking for archivists, though, is the speed at which the AI is now finding identifiable words. Going from a handful of legible words to full paragraphs of text, which took the AI several weeks, usually takes archivists years of painstaking study.",
    "questionText": "According to the passage, the parchment rolls ----.",
