    "passage": "New Zealand is listed as one of the world's most predator-threatened nations for native birds. In the spring, the struggle for survival faced by beloved species such as kiwi, kakapo, and takahe becomes increasingly acute as breeding season begins. The burrows and nests made by these birds are frequently destroyed by introduced predators such as stoats and rats. Nesting mothers are killed while defending their eggs. Nests are destroyed by unaware hikers, causing the birds to abandon breeding grounds. There is a good reason why conservationists describe spring as 'predator season'. For the last 35 years, the Coastal Guardians Trust has rescued, rehabilitated, and released native birds threatened by introduced predators. Each year, it responds to over 15,000 reports of injured wildlife. Predator season is its busiest period. During the spring months, the charity's purpose-built wildlife hospital operates around the clock, caring for hundreds of patients. Its work also helps other conservation groups: as a centre of excellence for native bird care, the hospital trains rangers and volunteers in the vital skills and protocols needed to ensure that injured birds get the best possible chance of release back into the wild. The last three springs have seen unprecedented numbers of patients. Increased outdoor tourism undoubtedly played a part as more visitors spent time exploring remote trails. Climate change is also a factor, with breeding patterns shifting noticeably, as birds nest earlier each year. To provide this level of care, the Coastal Guardians Trust relies on the generosity of the public as it receives limited government funding and frequently takes in patients from smaller, less-funded organisations.",
