    "passage": "In Athens's earliest days, two broad categories of citizens were identified: aristocrats and commoners. Aristocrats might be described as the landowning elite of Athenian society, for they generally held ancestral estates, political influence, and, perhaps most importantly, the right family lineage. Commoners, on the other hand, were for the most part the opposites of aristocrats: not as much land, influence, or lineage. Over time, some commoners and commoner families climbed the socioeconomic ladder, but the ascent was remarkably difficult. By the sixth century BCE, a prosperous merchant class began to emerge. Eventually, many merchants assumed considerable economic power in Athenian society, primarily because of their trading interests. The disparities between social classes were keenly felt throughout Athenian history. In politics, for example, it was very difficult for a self-made man — one from a family that had never produced influential magistrates — to strive successfully for high office. Two of the most prominent self-made men to reach the position of archon were Solon, in 594 BCE, and Themistocles, in 493 BCE. (An instructive contrast: self-made men were perceived as the opposites of eupatridae (well-born); hence the English words noble and nobility, which connote much the same for us today as did the word eupatridae for the ancient Athenians.)",
