    "passage": "Negotiators often make reference to variation in cultural expectations among the people they come in contact with during international deals. Comments often include the directness or indirectness of counterparts, and negotiators from high-context cultures are singled out as the most difficult ones to interact with. This is not surprising if we think that negotiators operate with a range of low-context and high-context communication styles who potentially display variation in their approach at all levels — tone, formality, and non-verbal signals. What makes their negotiations work, therefore, is not so much adherence to a single cultural norm but their ability to accommodate the unexpected and the use of collaborative skills, such as patience, flexibility, and active listening, to avoid breakdowns in cross-border deals. Studies have shown that communication in 'international business' is inherently intercultural, thus negotiators should respect not only multiple communication styles and identities, but also cultural diversity in cross-border settings. On that basis, because of the cultural hybridity of these contexts, researchers emphasise the building of trust, the co-construction of agreement, and the strategies used to resolve misunderstanding. They highlight the need to focus on the substance of the deal and understanding of shared interests rather than foregrounding rigid adherence to a single negotiating style.",
