    "passage": "Portrayed with wonder and splendour in countless ancient accounts, the Library of Alexandria was said to be one of antiquity's greatest achievements. It was a sprawling repository of knowledge, and in many ways it (22)---- the core ambitions of Hellenistic civilisation: scholarship, curiosity, and intellectual prestige. But what if its scale has been wildly exaggerated? After all, (23)---- the Library is mentioned in numerous later accounts, few contemporary documents from the time offer a precise description of it. Moreover, Strabo, a careful geographer who travelled extensively through Egypt, does not describe it in vivid detail in his own writings, (24)---- visiting Alexandria and discussing many of its other features at length. Surely, if Strabo had witnessed the full grandeur later writers claimed for the Library, he (25)---- at least a passage or two in his own accounts. As a result, scholars today still debate fiercely (26)---- just how vast and how organised the Library's holdings truly were. Some researchers consider the most extravagant claims pure legend, while others maintain that the Library was nonetheless an extraordinary centre of learning for its time.",
    "questionText": "(22). numaralı boşluğa uygun seçeneği işaretleyiniz.",
