    "passage": "Norway is one of only a handful of nations continuing to permit large-scale commercial fishing of Atlantic bluefin tuna, a species whose populations collapsed dramatically during the late twentieth century (the others include Japan and, to a lesser extent, Iceland). During the 1970s, Norwegian vessels caught tens of thousands of tonnes annually before the population crashed and the fishery was effectively closed. It reopened in 2014 under a strict quota system negotiated through the International Commission for the Conservation of Atlantic Tunas. Since then, catches have been carefully managed, and stocks have shown modest signs of recovery. But why does Norway continue this practice in the face of ongoing criticism from conservation groups? It is not primarily about profit. Bluefin tuna fishing employs a relatively small number of coastal communities, and the revenue it generates is a minor fraction of Norway's overall fishing industry. It is not really about tradition either, since large-scale bluefin fishing did not become widespread in Norwegian waters until well into the twentieth century, and many coastal Norwegians have never eaten it themselves, as most of the catch is exported to sushi markets in Japan. Rather, the practice persists partly because of the economic value placed on maintaining traditional fishing rights and partly because ending it unilaterally would set a precedent Norway's government is reluctant to establish for other fisheries. The picture is not entirely bleak, however. Scientific monitoring has become far more rigorous than it was decades ago, and conservationists note that the strict quota system, however contested, has coincided with a genuine, measurable rebound in the eastern Atlantic bluefin population.",
    "questionText": "One can conclude from the passage that Norway ----.",
