    "passage": "Throughout many Indigenous cultures of North America, body painting was an important means of expressing cultural identity and spiritual belief. Besides face and body decoration, adornment most often took the form of pigment derived from natural minerals. Such practices were common among Plains communities, but they were even more (22)---- developed among the Pacific Northwest peoples. Indeed, many of the pigments used carried symbolic meaning (23)---- origin, tied closely to specific clans, animals, or natural elements associated with each group. Within Pacific Northwest cultures, ceremonial body painting reached its highest expression in communities organised around elaborate social hierarchies. Historically, body painting served a variety of functions in these cultures (24)---- mere aesthetic display. For example, it signified rank (25)---- the complexity and colour of the patterns often reflected status within the community. Certain designs (26)---- only to individuals of high standing, but ordinary members of the community also painted themselves, typically with simpler patterns than those of the elite.",
