    "passage": "There is currently no single test that can be given to diagnose Sensory Processing Disorder (SPD) in children. Since some developmental and psychological conditions can present similarly to SPD, these should be considered and ruled out before a diagnosis of SPD is made. A comprehensive evaluation is necessary to establish a diagnosis of SPD. This includes information and observations from parents, occupational therapists, teachers, and paediatricians. Parents see their children at home and during daily routines. Classroom teachers can be of assistance since they see how well children cope with sensory input during school activities and how children interact with their peers. Occupational therapists can make structured sensory observations in clinical settings and interview the family. Paediatricians provide needed medical information and rule out other conditions. Completion of sensory profile checklists is also part of a comprehensive evaluation. The checklists rate the intensity of sensory-related behaviours and are completed by primary caregivers such as parents or guardians and classroom teachers. Items on the checklist include behaviours such as being oversensitive to textures, unusual reactions to sound, difficulty with balance, and excessive avoidance of certain movements. In addition to this information, a thorough evaluation is needed of the child's current level of motor, social, and emotional functioning. Careful consideration and review of all the information gathered is needed before the evaluation is complete.",
