    "passage": "Many bats treated at a wildlife rescue centre in Southeast Asia are carrying a novel strain of coronavirus. The discovery raises concerns about spillover to people and livestock. Human coronavirus infection is caused by several related viral strains. Bats can be infected with a similar strain called Rhinolophus coronavirus that causes a mild respiratory illness and can spread to other mammals. This strain has been detected in more than 200 bat species globally, but little is known about its prevalence in Southeast Asian bats specifically. To investigate, Amara Sok, at a wildlife research institute in Cambodia, and her colleagues, tested 412 bats belonging to 68 species that were admitted to a rescue centre in Siem Reap. In total, 21 percent of the bats tested positive to the coronavirus strain. Some were infected with the Rhinolophus strain and some with a related strain that typically affects civets. Two strains not previously seen in the region were also detected, as were two strains that are new to science. There are already some reports of the coronavirus spreading from Southeast Asian bats into people. In one case, nine wildlife rangers contracted a mild form of the virus through exposure to bat droppings while surveying a cave, and none required hospitalisation. \"At this stage, the health consequences of the new findings are unclear,\" says Sok. \"We're at the initial stages of tackling this issue, and we only have a very limited understanding of what allows the virus to jump between species.\"",
