    "passage": "In 2005, a group of enterprising engineers from a European space agency decided to investigate whether a class of building materials called phase-change compounds, which are used in insulation panels and thermal coatings, could be used to regulate temperature inside spacecraft. Results looked promising: In just 12 hours, one compound, for example, seemed to absorb nearly half of the excess heat it encountered. The agency was pleased. \"If we want to maintain stable conditions in enclosed environments, on Earth or in orbit,\" the report declared, \"we must take advantage of materials that store and release heat efficiently.\" The study sparked a great number of articles about the temperature-regulating potential of phase-change materials. Despite these, there was just one problem: The experiments worked best in tightly sealed, climate-controlled test chambers. However, most buildings are something a little more variable, and to replicate these effects in an ordinary structure, engineers estimate that you would need to install a considerable thickness of the material across every external wall. While these materials might not dramatically transform the temperature of a typical home, they offer a range of benefits that could help both practically (lower energy bills, reduced strain on heating systems) and environmentally (smaller carbon footprint). Better still, people do not have to redesign their entire house to enjoy some of these benefits.",
    "questionText": "Which of the following is true of the study carried out by the space agency?",
