    "passage": "Two approaches were taken to manage the availability of stored grain (foodstuff for both people and farm animals, such as wheat, barley, etc.) during the Iron Age. In regions in which the summers were particularly dry, such as the Anatolian plateau and parts of the Levant, rainfall was regularly so scarce and unpredictable that communities could not rely on a single annual harvest. The only option was to build large communal storage pits and bring the surplus grain from good years to sustain the population through lean ones. The archaeological evidence for this consists of extensive underground silos, some lined with clay to protect against moisture, found near the remains of settlements. Cuneiform records also quite often mention the rationing of grain during difficult years, and evidence for the practice of long-term storage is provided by the presence of large storage jars, used for grain, on numerous archaeological sites. The importance of grain is also reflected in the Hittite laws that deal with the theft and hoarding of grain from communal granaries. In the wetter, more temperate north, however, it was possible to rely on regular seasonal rainfall for consistent harvests. In regions such as parts of Northern Europe, the absence of large-scale communal storage meant that an alternative strategy had to be undertaken to ensure that communities would survive lean years. The archaeological record suggests that smaller household-level storage, supplemented by trade with neighbouring settlements, served much the same purpose as the large communal silos.",
