    "passage": "Nearly 40 percent of insect species could vanish within decades due to habitat loss, pesticide use, and climate change. Scientists are calling it a 'silent extinction'. Research suggests that certain regions (17)---- more than a third of their pollinator populations disappear over the last thirty years. Entomologist Fiona Marsh at the University of Sussex estimated population declines (18)---- 350 insect families between 1990 and 2020. The most common groups, (19)---- bees and butterflies, have been affected the most. More than 80 percent of the total insect decline (20)---- across just eight orders, including beetles, moths, and grasshoppers. Habitat fragmentation and chemical runoff are the leading causes of decline. Entomologists argue that people need to pay far more attention to these quiet losses (21)---- they can frequently be addressed through local, small-scale conservation efforts.",
