    "passage": "The earliest reliable method for identifying criminals by their physical traits was developed by Alphonse Bertillon, a French police clerk. This system recorded precise measurements of the body, such as the length of the forearm, the width of the skull, and other statistics, (22)---- distinguishing marks such as scars or birthmarks, along with a standardised photograph of the suspect. Although the system was time-consuming and (23)----, and could not always distinguish between two similar individuals, it was adopted by police departments across several countries. It suffered a serious setback in 1903 when a prisoner named Will West was processed, before officials discovered that another inmate held nearly identical Bertillon measurements and was, coincidentally, named William West. Bertillon's measurements were later supplemented (24)---- standardised photographs, which came to be known as 'mugshots'. Typically a photograph (25)---- both from the front and in profile. If the individual committed another offence later, the mugshot would be circulated widely, (26)---- he could be identified by officers on patrol or by the public.",
