    "subCategory": "Okuma Parçaları (Reading Comprehension)",
    "passage": "A compelling argument should have its greatest impact immediately after it is delivered, or so common sense suggests. It seems counter-intuitive to imagine that its persuasive power might actually grow stronger over time, yet this is precisely what the sleeper effect implies. An early finding from the Yale persuasion research programme was that messages advocating unpopular positions became more, rather than less, convincing several weeks after participants had first heard them. Hovland and Weiss reasoned that we initially link the message with cues about its source, such as how trustworthy or biased that source seemed. Of these two elements, memory of the message's content proves far more durable than memory of the source delivering it. If researchers were to measure the influence of a controversial message roughly a month later, the sleeper effect predicts that a message from a discredited source would become almost as persuasive as an identical message from a credible source, because the message endures while the association with its questionable source gradually fades. Eagly and Chaiken have described the sleeper effect, typically studied within the field of communication research, as a fragile phenomenon. Its existence has often been disputed, but it has nonetheless been reproduced under carefully controlled experimental conditions.",
    "questionText": "Based on the sleeper effect, over time, the persuasiveness of a message from a discredited source ----.",
