    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "solutionMethod": "✅ Doğru cevap: A) Clear and regular communication within a team is an important factor that not only ensures projects are completed on time but also reinforces trust among employees.\n\n🔎 Neden doğru? Doğru çeviri, 'X, Y'yi sağlamanın yanı sıra Z'yi de pekiştiren önemli bir unsurdur' yapısındaki iki sonucu (zamanında tamamlama ve güven) doğru sırayla ve doğru nedensellikle aktarır.\n\n❌ Diğer seçenekler neden yanlış?\n\nB) 'Although' ile kurulan zıtlık orijinal cümlede yoktur; anlam çarpıtılmıştır.\n\nC) 'only when' ifadesiyle orijinalde olmayan bir koşul eklenmiştir.\n\nD) Özne ve nesne yer değiştirmiş; orijinalde iletişim nedendir, güven değildir.\n\nE) Neden-sonuç ilişkisi tamamen tersine çevrilmiştir.\n\n🧠 YDS taktiği: 'not only... but also...' yapısını içeren cümlelerde, iki sonucun da doğru sırayla ve orijinal özneyle aktarılıp aktarılmadığına dikkat edilmelidir.\n\n📌 Öğrenilmesi gereken kelime/kalıp: reinforce (v.): pekiştirmek, güçlendirmek\n\n43-46: Okuma Parçası Sorusu\n\nOkuma Parçası Sorusu (Ayrıntı)\n\nNearly half of all electricity consumed in remote island communities is generated by diesel generators that are costly to run and vulnerable to fuel-supply disruptions. The objectives and practices of communities adopting renewable microgrids are extremely diverse. A small village may install a handful of solar panels and treat the system as a backup source of power during outages. A larger settlement may invest heavily in wind turbines and battery storage, aiming to eliminate diesel dependence altogether. Financial considerations are central to microgrid decisions: batteries are commonly sized to provide at least a full day of backup power, and solar arrays are positioned to maximise exposure throughout the year. Excess electricity may be sold back to a neighbouring grid and generate meaningful income. Many national governments have introduced subsidies that support the adoption of renewable microgrids in remote areas. Historically, the primary objectives were to lower fuel costs, reduce the risk of blackouts, and provide power to communities that lacked reliable grid access. Since the 2010s, resilience against extreme weather events has become an increasingly important consideration in some regions. Therefore, planning a microgrid for a specific community requires a detailed review of the community's needs, resources, and any regulatory constraints regarding land use.",
    "explanation": "✅ Doğru cevap: A) Clear and regular communication within a team is an important factor that not only ensures projects are completed on time but also reinforces trust among employees.\n\n🔎 Neden doğru? Doğru çeviri, 'X, Y'yi sağlamanın yanı sıra Z'yi de pekiştiren önemli bir unsurdur' yapısındaki iki sonucu (zamanında tamamlama ve güven) doğru sırayla ve doğru nedensellikle aktarır.\n\n❌ Diğer seçenekler neden yanlış?\n\nB) 'Although' ile kurulan zıtlık orijinal cümlede yoktur; anlam çarpıtılmıştır.\n\nC) 'only when' ifadesiyle orijinalde olmayan bir koşul eklenmiştir.\n\nD) Özne ve nesne yer değiştirmiş; orijinalde iletişim nedendir, güven değildir.\n\nE) Neden-sonuç ilişkisi tamamen tersine çevrilmiştir.\n\n🧠 YDS taktiği: 'not only... but also...' yapısını içeren cümlelerde, iki sonucun da doğru sırayla ve orijinal özneyle aktarılıp aktarılmadığına dikkat edilmelidir.\n\n📌 Öğrenilmesi gereken kelime/kalıp: reinforce (v.): pekiştirmek, güçlendirmek\n\n43-46: Okuma Parçası Sorusu\n\nOkuma Parçası Sorusu (Ayrıntı)\n\nNearly half of all electricity consumed in remote island communities is generated by diesel generators that are costly to run and vulnerable to fuel-supply disruptions. The objectives and practices of communities adopting renewable microgrids are extremely diverse. A small village may install a handful of solar panels and treat the system as a backup source of power during outages. A larger settlement may invest heavily in wind turbines and battery storage, aiming to eliminate diesel dependence altogether. Financial considerations are central to microgrid decisions: batteries are commonly sized to provide at least a full day of backup power, and solar arrays are positioned to maximise exposure throughout the year. Excess electricity may be sold back to a neighbouring grid and generate meaningful income. Many national governments have introduced subsidies that support the adoption of renewable microgrids in remote areas. Historically, the primary objectives were to lower fuel costs, reduce the risk of blackouts, and provide power to communities that lacked reliable grid access. Since the 2010s, resilience against extreme weather events has become an increasingly important consideration in some regions. Therefore, planning a microgrid for a specific community requires a detailed review of the community's needs, resources, and any regulatory constraints regarding land use.",
