    "passage": "The first positional numeral system was probably developed by Babylonian scribes perhaps as early as 1800 BCE in ancient Mesopotamia, and the earliest preserved examples, in cuneiform, come from the site of the ancient city of Nippur on the present-day Iraqi plain. With only sixty distinct symbols required, the Babylonian system was designed for the recording of astronomical observations and commercial transactions. Around 300 BCE, ancient Indian mathematicians developed a decimal place-value system and somewhat later introduced a symbol for zero, adapting the notation to the different demands of large-scale calculation. About a thousand years later, the Indian system, in turn, served as a model for Arab scholars of the Middle East, whose adaptation influenced European mathematicians to develop what is now called the Hindu-Arabic numeral system. Although the so-called Hindu-Arabic system is used for the writing of numbers in the great majority of the world's languages, the Roman numeral system, still used in some ceremonial and decorative contexts, was derived independently from ancient Etruscan symbols, which in many ways it still resembles.",
    "questionText": "It can be inferred from the passage that ----.",
