    "subCategory": "Okuma Parçaları (Reading Comprehension)",
    "passage": "Japan has one of the oldest continuous literary traditions in the world. The Manyoshu, a remarkable anthology of love, nature, and everyday life, for instance, goes back to the 8th century CE, among the earliest surviving collections of its kind in East Asia. Poets have always occupied a central place in Japanese culture – observers of the fleeting nature of the seasons and of human emotion. The poet Matsuo Basho (1644-1694), for example, is considered to be the figure who perfected the haiku, a form that captures a single moment with striking economy of language. He lived during the Edo period (1603-1868), an era of relative peace and flourishing arts, but he chose to abandon a comfortable life in the city to travel on foot through remote regions of Japan, often enduring hardship and uncertainty. In his travels, he composed poems that reflected both the beauty of the landscapes he encountered and the transience of life itself. 'There is Basho, there is Wordsworth, and there is Thoreau,' says literary scholar Helen Vendler. 'They are poets who found the extraordinary hidden within the ordinary.' This is considered to be part of the reason why Basho's poetry continues to resonate so widely, well beyond the borders of Japan.",
