    "passage": "A phobia is an intense, irrational, and persistent fear of a specific object, activity, or situation that leads a person to go to great lengths to avoid it, even though the danger is usually minimal or non-existent. Phobic fears are distinguished from ordinary caution in that they are wildly out of proportion to the actual threat posed. Typical phobias include a fear of enclosed spaces, a fear of heights, a fear of specific animals such as spiders or snakes, and a fear of social situations in which one might be judged by others. People with phobias may find themselves going to extraordinary lengths, such as taking long detours or missing important events, in order to avoid the source of their fear. Others try to confront their fears gradually, often with professional guidance. It is important to note that reasonable caution about genuinely hazardous situations – walking near a cliff edge, handling a venomous snake – is not a phobia. Although such caution can occasionally become excessive, it generally remains proportionate to the actual risk and can be adjusted with experience and information. Phobias, in contrast, persist regardless of evidence that the feared object or situation poses little real danger, and they can significantly limit a person's daily activities and opportunities.",
