    "passage": "Mary Anning was a British fossil collector and one of the pioneers of modern palaeontology. At the age of twelve, Anning helped her brother uncover the first correctly identified ichthyosaur skeleton, despite having received almost no formal education. This remarkable woman was among the first people to recognise how fossils could reveal the deep history of life on Earth. She spent much of her life (and likely most of her working hours) along the cliffs of Lyme Regis, searching for fossils that were exposed after storms eroded the coastline. In the end, she uncovered specimens so significant that they reshaped scientific understanding of prehistoric marine life. The fact that professional geologists of her era are now recognised as having relied heavily on her discoveries has nothing to do with any lack of skill or insight on Anning's part — it has everything to do with the fact that, as a working-class woman, she was excluded from the scientific societies that could have credited her properly. Formal palaeontology was only a nascent discipline, with no clear professional path for someone of her background. She had no university degree, and Anning certainly had no expectation of being named in the scientific papers that used her finds. Who could have imagined today's recognition of her contributions, achieved only decades after her death?",
