    "passage": "Applied across a wide range of fields, gene editing is the science of making precise, targeted changes to the DNA of a living organism. Originally, it dealt with the modification of single genes to correct simple genetic disorders, but this has become only one branch of the field, now referred to as precision genome engineering. The scope of gene editing has broadened to now include nearly all scientific efforts below the level of 'traditional genetics', thereby encompassing any techniques and tools dealing with targeted molecular changes. Due to the possibilities of gene editing in so many areas, from agriculture to medicine, the field has received increased attention from both investors and the public in recent years. 'CRISPR' refers to a set of DNA sequences found in bacteria that can be used, together with an associated protein, to cut DNA at precise locations; at this level, the components of genes and their sequences are specific enough to be physically located, removed, and replaced. At the technical level, gene editing is interested in using these molecular tools to correct mutations that cause inherited diseases. In theory, these techniques will be capable of remarkable precision and relatively low cost. On a more immediate and practical level, gene editing can be used to develop crops with new qualities such as drought resistance, higher yield, and pest resistance. However, the ethical and regulatory questions surrounding gene editing still outpace its current applications, if not its long-term promise.",
