    "passage": "Tai chi can be practised by those of almost any age and physical condition, although not all movements should be attempted by everyone. All that is needed is a flat, open surface large enough to move freely, and comfortable footwear. It is a good activity for those who cannot engage in high-impact sports, who do not enjoy competitive exercise, or have limited time for a gym routine. Instructors recommend practising on an empty stomach or waiting at least an hour after a light meal. Loose, breathable clothing should be worn. People with joint problems, heart conditions, or balance disorders should consult a doctor before beginning tai chi. Those with chronic conditions should find an instructor who is willing to adapt movements to their needs. Older adults can benefit greatly from tai chi, but should always start with a class designed for beginners. Certain postures should not be attempted during an episode of dizziness or vertigo. Beginners should practise with patience and focus when learning the forms, and not try to memorise entire sequences too quickly, as frustration could result. Some tai chi forms, like the 'single whip' and 'cloud hands', can be intricate and require balance, coordination, and gradual practice, so beginners should get the guidance of an instructor before attempting them independently.",
