    "passage": "To create fragrances that satisfy consumer expectations, perfume manufacturers need to know the relative contributions of the various aromatic qualities — top notes, heart notes, and base notes — to the overall scent of a product. Arguably, until this is known, it is difficult if not impossible to predict how a fragrance will be received by wearers. Such information can be used to guide fragrance development and ensure a consistent product by allowing measurement of the effects of different raw materials, blending methods, and ageing. The process of describing and measuring the aromatic qualities of scents is known as olfactory profiling (OP). To perform OP, small panels of typically eight or ten trained individuals receive extensive training. For a long period of time, the panellists try to learn to be consistent in their use of specific labels to describe scent qualities. Such training is necessary because of our generally limited ability to name odours accurately. Even with common household scents, correct identification can be as low as 40 percent. Despite being able to say that a scent is highly familiar, we are often at a loss to identify the correct name. This has been labelled the 'nose-tip' phenomenon. In addition, to describe how a scent evolves over time on skin, an entire vocabulary must be learned and applied appropriately. Importantly, training allows 'descriptor alignment' — essentially an agreement as to the meaning of aromatic terms and what constitutes examples of each. For example, the panel might need to agree that the term 'citrus note' refers to the scent of fresh citrus peel but not that of a processed citrus drink.",
