    "passage": "Aretha Franklin is the single most significant figure in soul music history, and it is hard to imagine soul music without her contributions. (17)---- transforming the way gospel-rooted vocals sounded and were perceived, she forever changed the way audiences relate to emotional expression in song. Emerging from the racially segregated American South in the 1950s, she promoted cultural pride by making deeply personal, African-American vocal traditions accessible to millions of listeners who (18)---- to it otherwise. Furthermore, in popularising soul music (19)---- a worldwide audience, Aretha almost single-handedly altered the parameters of the recording industry. By many estimates, she remains history's most influential female vocalist. Aretha certainly was not the first singer to draw on gospel roots; (20)----, she was the first to fuse elements of gospel, blues, and jazz into a commanding, unmistakable voice. Commanding and dignified yet warm and generous, private yet fiercely outspoken on civil rights, Aretha (21)---- the contradictions of her time and her genre, and this distinctive combination of musical traditions still moves audiences around the world.",
