    "passage": "What goes around comes around. Our deforestation practices do not just remove carbon sinks, they also alter regional rainfall patterns; however, it is a fact that most people are not aware of. Now it turns out that the changes in atmospheric chemistry they cause will feed back into the climate, further reducing precipitation. This was not always thought to be true. Climatologists had considered that trees removed were simply lost carbon storage, unable to affect regional weather. But a study now suggests that the reduced humidity it causes will rebound on the entire region, by acting on tiny airborne compounds called isoprenes. These compounds are produced by tropical trees and drift up into the air, seeding clouds and triggering rainfall. Elena Souza and her team at the Amazon Institute for Environmental Research gathered experimental data showing that remaining forest patches produce less isoprene as deforestation intensifies and canopy cover shrinks. After entering these figures into climate models, they estimate that 22 percent less isoprene will be released from the forest by the year 2100, compared to pre-deforestation levels. If deforestation rates double, which is plausible later this century, regional rainfall is expected to fall between 15 and 30 percent. Reduced cloud seeding will add between 4 and 9 percentage points to that figure, Souza estimates.",
