    "passage": "So far, only two types of mental exertion have been shown to improve or preserve overall cognitive ability. One is dance training. Experts state that dancing is not only a physical experience; it is a multisensory and cognitive experience requiring memory, coordination and quick decision-making. Practising complex choreography changes how the brain interprets and integrates a wide range of sensory and motor information, and dancing over a long period of time can change brain function and brain structure. The other is playing strategic board games. As for the role of strategic games in brain power, a study carried out among older adults revealed that working memory gets better when a new strategic game is practised intensively even if it is for a short time and it gets even better when more practice is done for longer. The results of the study confirm the cognitive benefits of strategic game-playing and give important and encouraging messages: firstly, even a short, intensive period of practice can show beneficial effects on some cognitive functions. Secondly, this effect can be maintained through continued practice. So, we can say that it is never too late to start a mental activity such as learning a new strategic game.",
