    "passage": "When farmers apply pesticides, some residue washes off fields during rainfall and enters streams. Once in the waterways, these chemicals are not filtered out naturally, and end up accumulating in aquatic life. To find out how agricultural residue finds its way into aquatic creatures, Priya Sharma at Monash University in Australia and her colleagues sampled mayflies, caddisflies, other insects and spiders from eight waterways in rural Victoria. Overall, the group detected 41 distinct pesticide compounds in the invertebrates. Organisms near intensive farmland contained the highest levels, but low levels were also detected in those from more pristine upstream sites. Sharma presumes agricultural pesticide residue probably accumulates in mayflies and caddisflies while they are underwater larvae, then transfers to spiders that feed on them after they emerge as adults. Other predators in the food web such as fish, platypuses, birds, bats and frogs may also become cross-contaminated, she says. Her team calculated that trout and platypuses feeding on insects near intensively farmed land would consume neonicotinoid doses up to a third of the level considered safe for aquatic organisms. The impacts of this are not certain, but previous studies have found that high-level exposure to neonicotinoids causes fish to become less vigilant about avoiding predators. Male birds also seem to sing less to females when exposed to the chemicals.",
