    "questionText": "(I) Brain activity measurements conducted by a Canadian university showed a clear neurological difference between reading a physical book and reading on a screen. (II) There was far more sustained attention in the brain waves of those who read printed pages, suggesting deeper comprehension and a stronger sense of narrative immersion. (III) E-readers are equipped with the opportunity of adjustable font sizes, built-in dictionaries and the option of instant highlighting to make reading more convenient. (IV) But a book's impact is not limited to short-term comprehension gains, or pleasurable tricks that print can play on your mind. (V) Reading printed books lowers the secretion of stress hormone 'cortisol' while increasing levels of a steroid called DHEA, which is associated with enhanced immune function, lower cholesterol and improved sleep quality.",
