    "passage": "According to conventional historical accounts, the modern era of democratic governance begins in 1776 with the advent of the American Revolution. This is, perhaps, not entirely (17)---- the many social changes inspired by that struggle. It is also because of the political transformation, marking the break between colonial rule and modern governance, (18)---- the American Revolution represents the beginning of modern democratic culture. Between 1777 and 1787, the newly formed states (19)---- several constitutions. These included documents establishing limited self-government, a loose confederation of states, and eventually a unified federal republic. All of these provided inspiration for reformers, republicans and abolitionists far (20)---- the borders of the new nation. Indeed, the legacy of these early constitutions eventually forced most European monarchs (21)---- constitutional reforms limiting their absolute powers. And of the many constitutions drafted across Europe and Latin America during the following half-century, a great number were the direct result of American influence.",
