    "passage": "The question of whether plants can be said to 'communicate' has been debated among biologists for decades. Some argue that plants merely react mechanically to their environment, while others contend that plants and animals show meaningful parallels in how they process and respond to information. The issue matters because it shapes how we define intelligence and agency in the natural world. Early sceptics such as strict mechanists insisted that responsiveness and awareness are entirely separate, and that plants, lacking a nervous system, cannot be said to process information in any meaningful sense. The laboratory experiments on volatile chemical signalling in the 1980s and 1990s were especially significant in this context: if plants could be shown to alter their chemistry in response to signals released by neighbouring plants, then even committed sceptics would have to concede that some form of information processing was occurring. It is now widely accepted that whatever signalling abilities plants possess are considerably simpler than animal communication. Still, many researchers are willing to accept that in certain cases, plants do exhibit coordinated, adaptive responses that resemble communication. Studies of root networks and mycorrhizal fungi have provided evidence of remarkably intricate resource-sharing behaviour between plants. Plant signalling science and chemical ecology have emerged as the fields dedicated to studying these phenomena. While there remain conceptual difficulties in defining these fields precisely, it appears increasingly plausible that many plants process environmental information in ways that can be rigorously studied.",
