    "passage": "Uncertainty is inherent in all scientific research. Laboratories, for example, face the risk of experiments yielding inconclusive results, and therefore insufficient data to support a hypothesis. There is also the risk that a rival team will publish similar findings first, and perhaps claim priority for a discovery. When a research group has received funding from a grant agency, there is a risk that renewal will be denied, and ongoing projects will become too costly to continue. Laboratories that rely on imported equipment are also exposed to supply-chain risk. Moreover, new research groups in particular may be exposed to the risk of depending on only one research question. Whereas large, well-established institutes often diversify their projects to spread risk, the survival of small laboratories is often linked to the success of one line of inquiry (the original basis for the lab's founding) or one specialised technique. A setback in that line of inquiry can lead to closure. It is essential that new laboratories remain alert to shifts in their field, and position themselves to adapt to those shifts. At its heart, risk in research is a strategic issue. Principal investigators must carefully weigh the operational risk of a new project, or the risks of a novel method, against potential breakthroughs or losses – in other words, the strategic consequences of action vs. inaction. Risk must be assessed and managed; and it poses a constant strategic challenge. Fortune favours the bold, but with careers and the survival of the laboratory at stake, caution cannot simply be abandoned.",
