    "questionText": "Exercise induces temporary muscle micro-damage, especially when we perform unfamiliar movements. ---- Many researchers think that soreness could be an unintended consequence of this repair process and other underlying physiological changes. Sports scientists propose that as muscle fibres rebuild themselves after exertion, they trigger inflammation, swelling and pain, all of which are essentially protective. But our adaptive bodies still convert all of this underlying activity into strength gains, often making the muscle more resilient than before.",
