    "questionText": "Toddlers can recognise the intentions of others, and use this information when responding to unfamiliar objects and people. As toddlers explore their world, they generally rely on the body language of their parents or caregivers to determine the safety or appropriateness of a particular action. ---- Are toddlers simply imitating their parent's physical responses, or do they actually experience a change in behaviour purely from the visual cues of the parent? What is known, however, is that as toddlers explore their environment, their behavioural responses to what they encounter are based on cues portrayed by their parent or primary caregiver.",
