    "questionText": "The earliest civilisations arose in regions where high agricultural productivity was possible, allowing dense populations to be supported. In the Old World, they emerged (17)---- the rivers of Mesopotamia, northern India, Egypt and northern China. Craft specialisation developed, trade flourished, writing began, and rulers were often given elaborate burials. (18)----, each civilisation also possessed unique features rooted in its own cultural background and environment. Life in Ancient Egypt developed around the Nile, which provided a steady water supply and fertile soil, and thus, (19)---- the surrounding desert regions, made farming possible. Navigation on the river was straightforward, since boats (20)---- northward with the current. From the 5th millennium BC, farming communities along the banks of the Nile gradually began to merge into a single cultural, political and economic unit. This process of unification was (21)---- by trading contacts and the need to control the Nile's floodwaters. To reap the benefits of the annual flooding, communities had to cooperate to build dams and flood basins.",
