    "passage": "During the 19th century, in most of Central and South America, a large majority of the population was of African origin. Most were enslaved and worked on plantations producing crops such as sugar and coffee for European markets. Slavery was abolished gradually and at varying rates across Central and South America starting in the early 1800s, with Brazil being the last country to outlaw it, in 1888. After abolition, freed slaves often joined the lower ranks of hierarchical societies where poverty and inequality were made worse by unfair land redistribution. In Latin America, independence did not bring widespread prosperity or good governance, but rather an imbalance of power and economic inequality. Military dictatorships were common, as were wars and civil conflicts. Meanwhile, Brazil experienced a 'rubber boom' from the 1880s, which fuelled unprecedented economic growth in Latin America through the extraction and sale of wild rubber from the Amazon jungle. However, this development was entirely funded by investment from Europe and North America and was largely designed to serve the needs of industrialised countries rather than local people.",
