    "passage": "We all start out as readers, don't we? First, it's picture books read to us by a parent. Then we begin to recognise sounds, letters, and words, and take our first steps toward becoming readers ourselves. Many of us caught the reading bug when we were young, and the books we read in childhood can stay with us forever. Reading is often the first piece of advice given to any aspiring writer. If you want to become a published author, it's a good idea to look at what has already been published — and at what is being published now. First, read the kind of material you yourself want to write. Whatever that is — romance, crime, sci-fi, thrillers, memoirs, sagas, fantasy, non-fiction — it makes sense to see what already exists in that genre. Second, look at what is being written outside your own genre. The more widely you read, the more you will learn. See how other writers do it, and work out what works for you and what does not. Learn how successful authors keep their readers turning the pages. Finally, do not ignore everyday material you cannot escape: newspapers and magazines, TV adverts, websites, social media, and so on. Ultimately, as a writer, you need to use words effectively to make every word count.",
