    "questionText": "(I) Wagner was a musical innovator who revolutionised opera by writing both the music and the words for his vast music-dramas — earlier composers had relied on librettists for the story. (II) He wanted to combine all the arts — music, poetry, drama, and painting — into what he called a Gesamtkunstwerk. (III) In addition, each major character, symbol, or place in his dramas had its own musical theme, or leitmotif, that accompanied it throughout the opera. (IV) The most extensive use of this technique appeared in his Ring Cycle, an epic story told across four operas. (V) In his essay On Conducting, he argued that a creative conductor should actively interpret the music rather than merely direct an orchestra like a traffic officer.",
