    "questionText": "(I) In 2002, the Department of Experimental Psychology at Oxford University took a group of 50 insomniacs and had them try different methods for falling asleep. (II) Those using the traditional method of counting sheep took slightly longer than average to fall asleep. (III) Some sleep research suggests that breaking up long working hours with brief 'power naps' of just a few minutes may be good for one's health. (IV) What worked best was imagining a tranquil scene such as a beach or a waterfall, which relaxes people and engages their imagination. (V) Counting sheep, on the other hand, is too boring or irritating to distract people from whatever is keeping them awake.",
