    "passage": "During the preschool years, hand-eye coordination develops to the point of near-independence in self-care tasks. A four-year-old is learning to use eating utensils competently and fasten even small buttons. Four-year-olds can also hold a pencil skilfully, copy geometric shapes and letters, and use scissors. By age five, a child's hand-eye coordination appears fairly advanced, though it will continue to be refined for several more years. A five-year-old approaches, grasps, and releases objects with precision and accuracy. He or she may play with the same toys as younger preschoolers but handles them with greater skill and purpose, and can complete a familiar jigsaw puzzle remarkably quickly. An important milestone at this stage is the ability to tie one's own shoelaces. By age six, a child's visual orientation shifts somewhat. Children this age and older shift their gaze more often than younger children do. They also tend to track the movement of an object rather than looking directly at it, a pattern linked to some six-year-olds' habit of using a finger to hold their place while reading. Even while absorbed in a task, they tend to look away frequently, even though their hands stay busy.",
