    "passage": "One might think that the shift from relying on the naked eye to using vision-enhancing instruments was among the greatest advances in human history. Yet no one deliberately set out to invent a telescope or a pair of spectacles. One of the most deeply held human beliefs was confidence in our unaided senses, especially eyesight itself. We still do not know exactly who invented spectacles, nor how or where. Everything we know suggests that they were invented largely by chance, by someone with no formal training in optics. Perhaps an older glassmaker producing glass panes for windows tested one of his discs by looking through it and was pleasantly surprised to discover that his vision improved. It seems unlikely that the inventor was a scholar, since academics of the period would surely have boasted about such a discovery, yet before the 1200s there is no record of any self-proclaimed inventor of this kind. The earliest word used to describe the device — an ordinary term for a small seed, chosen because of the lens's shape — was clearly not the sort of term a professor would use to describe an application of his optical theories. From the first documented use of spectacles before 1300 until the invention of the telescope roughly three centuries later, lenses attracted almost no attention from scholars.",
