    "subCategory": "Okuma Parçaları (Reading Comprehension)",
    "passage": "Child labour has historically meant putting children to work at adult tasks on farms, in factories, and in mines. Ironically, it both supported and obstructed the growth of universal public schooling in the United States. This issue did not become socially controversial until the Industrial Revolution began in the late 1700s. After several wars left Britain short of adult male workers, cotton mill owners began offering children free lodging and meals in company dormitories in return for their labour. Local authorities were pleased to see homeless children taken off the streets. For factory owners, employing children turned out to be one of the most profitable innovations of the era. The practice crossed the Atlantic to the United States, which likewise faced a shortage of adult male factory hands needed to produce food and tobacco, staples of the young nation's economy. At that time, most schooling was already reserved for landowners' children, so an entire generation of child labourers grew up unable to read by the 1830s. As the quality of manufactured goods declined and foreign sales of American products fell, many of the same industrialists who had once relied on child labour joined social reformers pushing to end the practice, backing universal compulsory schooling so that future workers would be able to read, write, and do arithmetic.",
