    "passage": "Organisations frequently tie pay rises to performance measures in order to motivate and hold on to their most valuable staff, which can also support the company's long-term success. Among the various forms of individual performance-related pay, merit-based pay is the most widely used. Under this approach, an employee's pay rise depends on a supervisor's evaluation of performance over a set period, typically the previous 12 months. The yearly pay cycle starts with setting performance goals, either by the supervisor alone or jointly with the employee. Progress is then tracked over the following months, and the supervisor scores the employee's performance against these goals on a fixed scale. Each score is tied to a specific pay-rise percentage, meaning higher ratings translate into larger increases. The actual size of the increase can also depend on where the employee sits within the pay band. Typically, employees near the bottom of the pay band who receive high ratings get a bigger rise than those near the top of the band with an equally high rating. In some cases, this is intended to let an employee reach the top of the pay band right around the time they become eligible for promotion. The catch is that not every employee supports this system. Studies have shown that merit-based pay appeals more to certain occupations, such as managers, and to particular demographic groups — white-collar workers, higher earners, and younger staff — but it may not suit every occupation or every organisation.",
