    "questionText": "Nothing energises a household like the smell of fresh toast in the morning. In the autumn of 1919, American engineer Charles Strite installed an automatic pop-up mechanism in a toaster, bringing the convenience of hands-free toasting to his customers. Strite, a factory maintenance worker in Stillwater, Minnesota, (22)---- the pop-up toaster as a way to help cafeteria workers stop burning bread on busy mornings. Strite decided (23)---- his invention literally to a wider market by patenting the mechanism and licensing it to manufacturers. (24)---- using a spring-loaded timer and a heating element, he connected the toaster's mechanism to a simple lever that shut off automatically. Along the way he adjusted the design to suit different bread thicknesses, (25)---- creating the first mass-produced automatic toaster. The pop-up toaster, now a kitchen staple, is found in nearly 80 per cent of US homes and (26)---- much of Europe.",
