    "passage": "Nostalgia is a sentimental longing for the past. In nostalgic reflection, a person recalls a mental image of a past experience or a period that he or she associates with warmth and meaning. The person experiencing nostalgia may use these mental images to cope with a difficult present, to feel a sense of continuity, or to satisfy a longing for connection. Although most psychologists now view nostalgia as generally beneficial and adaptive, this was not always the case. In the 1700s, for example, medical texts used for training physicians described nostalgia as a dangerous illness that could even prove fatal to soldiers stationed far from home. Nostalgic feelings occur in patterns influenced by mood and significant life transitions, or they can be triggered by a sensory input such as a smell, a song, a taste, or a familiar object. Psychologists estimate that most people experience nostalgia at least once a week, and nostalgic episodes peak during periods of stress or loneliness. Nostalgic reflection first occurs for most people during adolescence, sometime around the age of twelve, and these early experiences set the pattern for adult nostalgic tendencies. People who have positive, comforting early memories usually continue to draw on these types of mental images into adulthood. Nostalgia becomes a resource for emotional regulation, meaning-making, or social connection. On the other hand, people whose early memories are largely negative or painful are more likely to experience sadness when reflecting on the past, and this pattern will carry over into adulthood as well. A person's nostalgic reflection may take a visible or shared form — the person talks about these memories with others while reminiscing, and may even revisit places connected to them. After early adulthood, however, nostalgic reflection tends to become more private — the person no longer always shares it but continues to experience it internally.",
