    "subCategory": "Okuma Parçaları (Reading Comprehension)",
    "passage": "More folklore surrounds the wolf than almost any other predator in the wild. This is partly a symptom of their elusive nature – wild wolves rarely appear near human settlements, and the temptation to exaggerate a single sighting into a pack of dozens is difficult to resist. Wolves are also, by nature, secretive. Scientists still know relatively little about wolf pack hierarchies in truly wild, undisturbed populations; a full record of a pack's internal politics has rarely been observed from beginning to end. One of the most persistent wolf myths is that the animal, driven purely by hunger, will readily attack and kill humans whenever the opportunity arises. \"Almost entirely false,\" says Karin Lindqvist, director of the Nordic Predator Research Unit in Oslo, Norway. After tracking more than 800 wolf-human encounters over eight years, she states that wolves typically flee at the first sign of a person, and direct, unprovoked attacks are exceptionally rare. Lindqvist points out that wolves are cautious and highly social animals, which is something most people fail to appreciate. When wolves do approach human settlements, they are usually searching for easy food sources such as livestock or refuse, not hunting people. They tend to avoid direct contact whenever possible because confrontation carries a high risk of injury. To add more, wolves are surprisingly selective about which prey they pursue. Chasing an unsuitable target wastes energy that the pack cannot easily recover, and an injury sustained during a hunt could compromise the survival of the entire group for weeks.",
    "questionText": "Which could be the best title for this passage?",
