    "passage": "In Mikael Sørensen's family, there is a tradition of pushing boundaries. His grandmother, Ingrid, was among the first women to pilot a glider across the North Sea. His father, Anders, was the first to chart a previously unmapped stretch of Arctic seabed using early sonar equipment. And in 2005, Sørensen completed the first non-stop, human-powered crossing of the English Channel by pedal boat. This achievement, combined with a growing concern about ocean pollution, inspired the Danish engineer to dream of circling the globe in a boat that produces no waste. The result is the Ocean Pioneer, a vessel equipped with more than 12,000 photovoltaic panels. Thanks to advanced battery cells that store energy efficiently, it is the first solar-powered boat that can maintain full speed through the night. \"If we want to solve our plastic and emissions problems,\" says Sørensen, an engineer by training, \"we need to rethink our relationship with disposable materials and invest in circular technologies. The Ocean Pioneer is really a way to show that those technologies are ready.\"",
