    "questionText": "(I) The idea of a person's genetic code as entirely private information has persisted for decades. (II) Today, however, this assumption might no longer hold. (III) Advanced sequencing technology can now decode an individual's entire genome within hours, at a steadily falling cost. (IV) Home DNA-testing kits are vulnerable to data breaches, and researchers suggest that the same vulnerability applies to genetic data held by healthcare providers. (V) Whereas these advances hold great promise for research and medicine, they raise a fundamental ethical, legal and social challenge: determining under what conditions it is legitimate to access another person's genetic information.",
