    "questionText": "(I) The conventional wisdom is that it is a molecule's specific shape that allows our tongues to distinguish between different tastes. (II) But there is new evidence that it is actually the frequency of a molecule's vibration, a quantum effect, that is all-important. (III) Most taste researchers believe that receptors on our tongue act like a lock that can only be activated by a molecule of a specific shape - the 'key'. (IV) Molecules are collections of atoms that can be thought of as being connected by springs that can vibrate. (V) The trouble is, some molecules with different shapes produce similar tastes.",
